ity: Before setting viewer.trackedEntity, assign a fixed local camera offset directly to the picked entity. Add this line:\npickedEntity.viewFrom = new Cesium.Cartesian3(150000.0, -150000.0, 150000.0);\n(This anchors the camera exactly 150 km away on the X, Y, and Z axes relative to the satellite, ensuring it always looks down at the object with Earth in the background).\n\nSimplify the Lock: Remove the complex viewer.flyTo() animation block entirely. Just use:\n\nJavaScript\npickedEntity.viewFrom = new Cesium.Cartesian3(150000.0, -150000.0, 150000.0);\nviewer.trackedEntity = pickedEntity;\nCesium will automatically handle the smooth flight tra\n<truncated 542 bytes>\n5. \"The 3D orbit viewer is looking incredibly professional. Now, I want to add an 'Interactive Temporal Scrubber' to the bottom of the 3D view so users can manually fast-forward and rewind time to watch the orbital mechanics in action.\n\nPlease update index.html and app.js with the following:\n\nSleek Scrubber UI: Add a custom HTML range slider floating at the bottom center of the '3D ORBIT VIEWER' tab (use glassmorphism: bg-black/60 backdrop-blur-md rounded-full px-6 py-4 w-1/2 absolute bottom-8 left-1/2 transform -translate-x-1/2 z-40).\n\nSlider Configuration: The slider should range from -12 to +12 (representing hours from the current time), with a step of 0.1. Default value is 0.\n\nDynamic Time Label: Above the slider track, display the current simulated time in a glowing, monospace font (e.g., T-Plus 02:30:00 | UTC: 2026-09-24 04:00:00).\n\nCesium Clock Integration: Write vanilla JS to listen to the slider's input event. When the user drags the slider, programmatically update viewer.clock.currentTime.\n\nBase the\n<truncated 457 bytes>\n6. The satellites are static points. They aren't moving when the timeline scrubber is used. We need to implement real frontend orbit propagation so they physically move along their trajectories over time, and we need to upgrade the Time Machine with professional playback controls and visual collision alerts.\n\nPlease update index.html and app.js with these exact implementations:\n\n1. Real-Time Orbit Propagation (index.html & app.js)\n\nIn index.html, import the standard frontend orbital mechanics library: <script src=\"[https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/satellite.js/4.0.0/satellite.min.js](https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/satellite.js/4.0.0/satellite.min.js)\"></script>.\n\nIn app.js, when creating a satellite entity, do not set a static position. Instead, initialize a new Cesium.SampledPositionProperty().\n\nWrite a loop that iterates from Date.now() to Date.now() + 24 hours in 2-minute increments. Inside the loop, use satellite.js to propagate the TLE data into Cartesian coordinates for that specific times\n<truncated 1293 bytes>\n7. \"The 'Jump to TCA' button shows a crude browser alert: 'No conjunction selected. Click a row in Live Tracking first.' Let's make this feature bulletproof, functional, and cinematic.Please update app.js and index.html with the following:Smart Fallback (Never Show Alert):If no conjunction is currently selected when 'JUMP TO TCA' is clicked, do not trigger a browser alert. Instead, automatically default to the highest-risk conjunction (the CRITICAL event, e.g., COSMOS 2251 DEB vs STARLINK-1007).Precision Clock Jump:Parse the TCA timestamp into a Cesium.JulianDate.Set viewer.clock.currentTime = tcaJulianDate.Pause the clock (viewer.clock.shouldAnimate = false) so the user can inspect the exact closest approach moment.Update the timeline slider position and time label so they reflect the exact TCA time.Dual-Object Dynamic Framing:Instead of locking to just one object, calculate the midpoint between the two conjunction entities at TCA.Smoothly fly the camera (viewer.camera.flyTo) to a vantage point that frames b\n<truncated 753 bytes>\n8. \"Navigating the 3D globe with a mouse or trackpad is feeling clunky. Let's add a virtual on-screen joystick (like a drone controller) to smoothly